{"version":1,"slug":"photosynthesis","topic":"How does photosynthesis work?","level":"beginner","title":"How Plants Make Their Own Food","summary":"You will understand how plants use sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to build sugar and release oxygen.","scopeNote":null,"scenes":[{"id":"intro","kind":"intro","title":"How does photosynthesis work?","segments":[{"id":"intro-0","narration":"Great question! How does photosynthesis work? Let's work it out together.","sig":"9rufvMunkw79Bqp6W3jBU6pjd8wsgDVI","actions":[]},{"id":"intro-1","narration":"Every single day, trees quietly build themselves out of thin air and sunlight. How do they pull off that magic trick?","sig":"C3a1Dzm4SeKSHOTAFauqhXtfclZAFKzp","actions":[{"id":"hero","kind":"icon","cue":"Every single day,","text":"Solar-powered food factory","items":null,"icon":"leaf","at":{"col":0,"row":1,"w":4,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"intro-2","narration":"Here's our plan. First, The Plant's Solar Kitchen. Then, Inside the Green Leaf. And finally, Why It Matters to Us.","sig":"8zzcWXyl8J-b3dorjRRFyTFHuNP_XFnu","actions":[{"id":"agenda","kind":"timeline","cue":"Here's our plan","text":null,"items":["1: The Plant's Solar Kitchen","2: Inside the Green Leaf","3: Why It Matters to Us"],"icon":null,"at":{"col":5,"row":1,"w":7,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c1","kind":"chapter","title":"The Plant's Solar Kitchen","segments":[{"id":"c1-0","narration":"Most people think plants eat food from the soil, just like we eat from a plate. Actually, plants are solar-powered chefs that make their own food.","sig":"cIowzyA0Y_0cqP0_6YmG8tWCxu861GBy","actions":[{"id":"soil","kind":"box","cue":"food from the soil","text":"Food from soil","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":3,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"red","expr":null},{"id":"x1","kind":"cross_out","cue":"Actually, plants are","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"soil","color":"red","expr":null},{"id":"leaf","kind":"icon","cue":"solar-powered chefs","text":"Plant Chef","items":null,"icon":"leaf","at":{"col":5,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-1","narration":"To cook up a meal, a leaf only needs three simple ingredients. First, it catches energy from sunlight.","sig":"CfvCVNSItsQLIK6-zoGLR_HRngJnNOzk","actions":[{"id":"sun","kind":"icon","cue":"energy from sunlight","text":"Sunlight","items":null,"icon":"sun","at":{"col":1,"row":3,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"a1","kind":"arrow","cue":"catches energy","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"leaf","target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"from sunlight","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"sun","color":"orange","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-2","narration":"Next, the roots drink up water from the ground, and tiny pores in the leaf breathe in carbon dioxide from the air.","sig":"KV6rwMKK7hwFdcqsJtlrpwffE_xHqdEd","actions":[{"id":"water","kind":"icon","cue":"drink up water","text":"Water","items":null,"icon":"droplet","at":{"col":5,"row":4,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"a2","kind":"arrow","cue":"drink up water","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"water","to":"leaf","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"t1","kind":"trace","cue":"from the ground","text":null,"items":null,"icon":"droplet","at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"a2","color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"co2","kind":"box","cue":"carbon dioxide","text":"Carbon Dioxide","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":9,"row":1,"w":3,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"a3","kind":"arrow","cue":"from the air","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"co2","to":"leaf","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c1-3","narration":"Put those three together, and the leaf builds its own sugar.","sig":"_C15mLxw-4uq7-v-cP071t_zJDW2Moji","actions":[{"id":"sugar","kind":"box","cue":"its own sugar","text":"Sugar (Food)","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":9,"row":3,"w":3,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"a4","kind":"arrow","cue":"builds its own","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"leaf","to":"sugar","target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"c1","kind":"check","cue":"own sugar","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"sugar","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c2","kind":"chapter","title":"Inside the Green Leaf","segments":[{"id":"c2-0","narration":"Now that we know what goes into the leaf, let us zoom inside to see where the cooking happens. Deep inside the leaf cells are tiny green factories called chloroplasts, packed with chlorophyll.","sig":"NUphhN4DxxyCxdSLwq_sDGyMfsa5Rx-y","actions":[{"id":"chloro","kind":"box","cue":"tiny green factories","text":"Chloroplast (chlorophyll)","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":4,"row":1,"w":4,"h":3},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"p1","kind":"pulse","cue":"packed with chlorophyll","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"chloro","color":"green","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-1","narration":"Chlorophyll acts like a solar panel, capturing light energy from the sun. It uses that energy to split water molecules apart.","sig":"Fi2Z8gL9JVOKWSOpT51C4s2OJOemHsGo","actions":[{"id":"sun","kind":"icon","cue":"capturing light energy","text":"Light energy","items":null,"icon":"sun","at":{"col":0,"row":0,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"a1","kind":"arrow","cue":"capturing light energy","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"sun","to":"chloro","target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"water","kind":"icon","cue":"split water molecules","text":"Water","items":null,"icon":"droplet","at":{"col":0,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"a2","kind":"arrow","cue":"split water molecules","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"water","to":"chloro","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-2","narration":"Next, it combines parts of that split water with carbon dioxide from the air. Together, they form glucose, a simple sugar that feeds the plant.","sig":"tJl5oQ0JN5hvmDyAPr2IzLPCLAM-Ozc3","actions":[{"id":"co2","kind":"box","cue":"carbon dioxide","text":"Carbon dioxide","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":0,"row":4,"w":2,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"a3","kind":"arrow","cue":"carbon dioxide","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"co2","to":"chloro","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"sugar","kind":"box","cue":"form glucose","text":"Glucose (sugar)","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":9,"row":1,"w":3,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"a4","kind":"arrow","cue":"form glucose","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"chloro","to":"sugar","target":null,"color":"green","expr":null}]},{"id":"c2-3","narration":"And what is left over? Fresh oxygen gas, which drifts right out of the leaf into the air.","sig":"JKDeTOY2j8vMYAcCHUcuUUaqu3vpc8YV","actions":[{"id":"o2","kind":"box","cue":"Fresh oxygen gas","text":"Oxygen","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":9,"row":3,"w":3,"h":1},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"a5","kind":"arrow","cue":"Fresh oxygen gas","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"chloro","to":"o2","target":null,"color":"ink","expr":null},{"id":"t1","kind":"trace","cue":"drifts right out","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"a5","color":"ink","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"c3","kind":"chapter","title":"Why It Matters to Us","segments":[{"id":"c3-0","narration":"So a leaf makes sugar for itself, and releases oxygen into the air. That oxygen is what you and every animal breathe in to stay alive.","sig":"47OOPzkWeTAD9SMpo2OShnPB4jj6c2kU","actions":[{"id":"plant","kind":"icon","cue":"a leaf makes sugar","text":"Plants","items":null,"icon":"tree-deciduous","at":{"col":1,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"animal","kind":"icon","cue":"you and every animal","text":"Animals & Us","items":null,"icon":"user","at":{"col":9,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"o2_arrow","kind":"arrow","cue":"breathe in","text":"Oxygen","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"plant","to":"animal","target":null,"color":"blue","expr":null},{"id":"o2_trace","kind":"trace","cue":"breathe in","text":null,"items":null,"icon":"wind","at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"o2_arrow","color":"blue","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-1","narration":"When we breathe out, we release carbon dioxide, which plants take right back in. Together, plants and animals form a giant recycling loop.","sig":"SMP-PoK4VkZ4nIApC3tnFmy6vj2XeWrG","actions":[{"id":"co2_arrow","kind":"arrow","cue":"release carbon dioxide","text":"Carbon dioxide","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"animal","to":"plant","target":null,"color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"co2_trace","kind":"trace","cue":"take right back in","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"co2_arrow","color":"orange","expr":null},{"id":"loop_note","kind":"note","cue":"giant recycling loop","text":"Nature's recycling loop","items":null,"icon":null,"at":{"col":4,"row":4,"w":4,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"purple","expr":null}]},{"id":"c3-2","narration":"And it is not just the air. Every apple, grain of wheat, and bite of food you eat traces back to sunlight captured by a plant.","sig":"NolYr3Sfla46uBNTuRlGmf21rG_f3RI6","actions":[{"id":"food","kind":"icon","cue":"Every apple","text":"Food & Energy","items":null,"icon":"apple","at":{"col":5,"row":0,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"food_arrow","kind":"arrow","cue":"traces back to","text":"Feeds","items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":"plant","to":"food","target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"food_pulse","kind":"pulse","cue":"captured by a plant","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"food","color":"green","expr":null}]}]},{"id":"recap","kind":"recap","title":"Recap","segments":[{"id":"recap-0","narration":"So, plants use sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to build sugar inside green chloroplasts, releasing the oxygen we breathe. Everything we eat and breathe connects back to leaves. What questions do you have?","sig":"NV0-QL9J46HMAtQBDu3sH09MQTGoA_Hc","actions":[{"id":"takeaways","kind":"list","cue":"plants use sunlight","text":null,"items":["Sunlight, water, and air make sugar","Chloroplasts release fresh oxygen gas","Plants feed us and our air"],"icon":"circle-check","at":{"col":1,"row":1,"w":6,"h":4},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"leaf_recap","kind":"icon","cue":"Everything we eat","text":"Photosynthesis","items":null,"icon":"leaf","at":{"col":8,"row":2,"w":2,"h":2},"from":null,"to":null,"target":null,"color":"green","expr":null},{"id":"p_recap","kind":"pulse","cue":"connects back to","text":null,"items":null,"icon":null,"at":null,"from":null,"to":null,"target":"leaf_recap","color":"green","expr":null}]}]}],"quiz":[{"question":"Where do plants actually get the food they need to grow?","options":["They absorb it from the soil","They make it from sunlight, water, and air","They drink it through their bark","They catch tiny insects in leaves"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Plants are solar-powered chefs that combine sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to build their own sugar.","chapter":1},{"question":"What job does chlorophyll do inside a leaf's chloroplasts?","options":["It pumps water up from the roots","It stores extra sugar for winter","It captures sunlight to split water","It blocks carbon dioxide from entering"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Chlorophyll acts like a solar panel, capturing light energy to split water molecules and start making glucose.","chapter":2},{"question":"How do plants and animals form a recycling loop?","options":["Plants release oxygen, and animals breathe out carbon dioxide","Animals make sunlight, and plants make soil","Plants eat animals, and animals eat leaves","Both plants and animals only breathe in oxygen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plants release the oxygen we breathe in, and we breathe out the carbon dioxide that plants need.","chapter":3}],"suggestions":["How do plants breathe at night?","Why do leaves change color?","How do roots drink up water?"],"models":{"lesson":"google/gemini-4-hydrogen","voice":"google/gemini-3.8-flash-tts"},"createdAt":"2026-11-17T17:37:28.894Z","audioBase":"/samples/photosynthesis"}